      {
        "id": "story",
        "type": "story",
        "provenance": "original",
        "title": {
          "en": "Gauss and the quick sum"
        },
        "text": {
          "en": "A story says a boy was asked to add 1 to 100 and answered at once. He paired 1 with 100, 2 with 99 and so on. That idea gives us the sum formula for every AP."
        }
      },
      {
        "id": "explain",
        "type": "explanation",
        "provenance": "original",
        "keepEnglishTerms": [
          "1 + ... + 100 = 5050",
          "S = n/2 (a + l)",
          "S = n/2 [2a + (n-1)d]",
          "a = 8, d = -5",
          "an = Sn - S(n-1)",
          "arithmetic mean",
          "choose formula",
          "pairing"
        ],
        "text": {
          "en": "Pair the first and last terms: each pair has the same sum, so we get n by 2 pairs of the sum a + l.\nThe sum of the first n terms of an AP is S = n by 2 times (2a + (n - 1) d).\nIf the last term l is known, the sum is S = n by 2 times (a + l).\nAdd 1 to 100: n = 100, a = 1, l = 100, so S = 50 x 101 = 5050.\nFind the sum of the first 22 terms of 8, 3, -2: a = 8, d = -5, S = 11 (16 - 105) = -979.\nThe nth term can be found from sums: a_n = S_n - S_(n-1).\nIf a, b, c are in AP then b = (a + c)/2, and b is called the arithmetic mean of a and c.\nPick the formula that matches what is given: use S = n/2 (a + l) when the last term is known."
        }
      },
